%erg 0.1
Title: erg-pr-merge dies on pending CI instead of arming auto-merge
Created: 2026-10-10
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-10-10T16:30Z claude created
2026-10-10T16:45Z claude note Option 2 rewritten at the author's request: no shell sleep loop and no foreground --watch (they hang; see 0889); a one-shot status command replaces it; portability constraints and criteria added.

--- body ---
## Context

`skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` queues the merge with `--auto` and documents that
the PR "lands when required checks pass" (script step 3, line 373: "--auto lets
GitHub gate on required checks itself"). Before reaching that step it refuses:
lines 144-149 count checks whose status is not COMPLETED and
`die "CI has N check(s) still running — wait for completion"`.

The two behaviours contradict each other. On 2026-10-10, PR #1345 (a ticket-only
change) was merged by `/merge` seconds after opening; the script refused with 10
checks running. The agent ended its turn, no later caller re-ran the script, and
the PR sat open for about an hour with all 10 checks green after roughly two
minutes (slowest: pytest-guard, 1m36s). A refusal in a multi-step flow (raid,
then roar) ends the flow at that point; the `/roar` that should follow never
runs.

Related: ticket 1081 (the missing `Ticket:` line is the other `/merge` bounce),
ticket 1045 (a local gate; the author decided on 2026-10-06 that waiting is
preferred to bypassing the forge's required checks). Not read in detail: the
rest of the script's watch-then-merge fallback (lines ~380-460).

## Options, priced (estimates, not measurements)

1. Track typical CI duration and delay the check. About 0.5 author-hour. A wrong
   guess repeats this failure; run times vary (a queued runner breaks it).
2. Get the outcome without the caller waiting. Not a shell sleep loop, and not a
   foreground `gh pr checks --watch`: a polling loop in Bash is the shape that
   hangs (ticket 0889 measured 105 agent-written waiters in 551 sessions and two
   stranded processes; `scripts/guard-pgrep-waiter.sh` denies the pgrep form).
   `gh ... --watch` is the same poll inside gh and hangs when checks never
   register (the script already handles that race, lines ~425-445). Two shapes
   survive these objections:
   a. A one-shot status command (working name `erg-pr-status N`): reads the
      forge once and exits 0 merged, 1 failed, 2 pending. It never waits. The
      caller (raid, roar, the next turn) asks again at its next step, and a
      pending answer is a normal state, not an error.
   b. A runtime-native background wake (Claude Code `run_in_background`, which
      re-invokes the caller on exit) around that one-shot command, with a hard
      timeout. Optional; only where the runtime has such a mechanism.
   About 1 author-hour for (a). (b) adds nothing to (a) except prompt wake-up.
3. Local CI only. 2 to 4 author-hours plus a policy change; it is ticket 1045's
   scope and the forge gate stays closed, so it is out of scope here.
4. Arm auto-merge immediately (drop the PENDING `die`, keep the FAILING one) and
   let the forge gate on required checks. Smallest change; the caller can no
   longer tell merged from pending without a later check, so pair it with option
   2 for callers that need the outcome (raid, roar).

## Portability constraints

The fix must work on every runtime and machine the harness serves (Claude Code,
Pi, Codex, Vibe; padme, doudou, CI), so it rests on git, the forge CLI and exit
codes only:
- no sleep or pgrep loop, no `timeout`-wrapped retry loop, no `--watch` in
  foreground; the only waiting is the forge's own auto-merge, which needs none
  of the caller's time;
- no dependence on a runtime's background-task or wake-up feature for
  correctness (that is an optimisation per runtime, shape 2b);
- no GNU-only flags (`date -d`, `sed -i`, `timeout`) and no assumption about the
  shell beyond what `erg-pr-merge` already uses; check it runs on doudou's
  system Python and PATH (a non-login `ssh` omits `~/.local/bin`, journal
  2026-10-09);
- the idempotence rule in `skills/merge/SKILL.md` still holds: a re-run after
  the close commit is pushed must not close twice.

Recommendation: option 4 plus option 2a (the one-shot status) for callers that
must continue; 2b only as a per-runtime extra. Case against: a PR armed while checks run can merge before a late-registering check
that is not in the ruleset's required set; confirm the required set covers every
check that matters before dropping the guard.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Reproduce: a test (or recorded run) shows the script returning non-zero
      on a PR whose checks are pending, and the chosen behaviour instead.
- [ ] The chosen option is implemented in `skills/merge/erg-pr-merge` with its
      test, and `skills/merge/SKILL.md` describes the actual behaviour.
- [ ] The `/raid` and `/roar` callers get the outcome (merged, failed or
      pending) from a one-shot call, with no loop and no foreground wait; a
      test greps the new code and the skills for `sleep`, `pgrep -`, `--watch`
      and retry loops.
- [ ] Portability: the new path runs on padme and doudou and under each
      supported runtime without a runtime-specific feature.
- [ ] `make check` passes.

Not part of this ticket: implementing it now (author instruction 2026-10-10).
